## Knowledge
[evidence] `.ok()` converts `Result<T, E>` to `Option<T>`: `Ok(value)` becomes `Some(value)`, and `Err(error)` becomes `None`. Thus the proposed API discards the `ParseError` value, including which field was invalid.

## Model
**Review contract:** Compare the supplied signatures and caller against the proposed conversion. Do not infer an unstated release policy or claim execution.

**Minimal fixture:**
```rust
enum ParseError {
    InvalidField,
}

struct Record;

fn decode(_input: &str) -> Result<Record, ParseError> {
    Err(ParseError::InvalidField)
}

// Current API:
fn parse(_input: &str) -> Result<Record, ParseError> {
    decode(_input)
}

// Proposed API:
fn parse(input: &str) -> Option<Record> {
    decode(input).ok()
}

fn caller(text: &str) -> Result<Record, ParseError> {
    let record = parse(text)?;
    match decode(text) {
        Err(ParseError::InvalidField) => Err(ParseError::InvalidField),
        Ok(_) => Ok(record),
    }
}
```

[inference] This fixture illustrates the mismatch, but the caller body as written is not a valid demonstration of the original caller unchanged: with the proposed `Option` return, `parse(text)?` cannot propagate its absence as `ParseError` from a function returning `Result<Record, ParseError>`. The separate `ParseError::InvalidField` match also cannot match an error returned by the new `parse`; the error is no longer available there. The `decode` match in this illustrative fixture is only a workaround that reparses and does not restore the original API contract.

