[inference] Changing the public return type breaks the described downstream usage: `?` no longer propagates a `ParseError`, and matching `ParseError::InvalidField` from `parse` is no longer possible. Under common Rust 0.x versioning practice, a breaking API change may be released in a later minor version, such as 0.9; the fixture does not specify the crate’s exact compatibility policy, so that policy remains uncertain.

[proposal] Preserve `Result<Record, ParseError>` if callers need actionable parse errors. If discarding error details is intentional, document the API change and decide the appropriate version under the crate’s stated policy.

